---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FAQ | salarynegotiationcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about salary negotiation: when to negotiate, how much to ask for, and how to handle counteroffers.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>When should I bring up salary?</h2>
  <p>After you have an offer — not before. In the interview process, avoid disclosing your current or target salary as long as possible. Many states (California, New York, Colorado, others) prohibit employers from asking for salary history. Once you have an offer, negotiate: you have maximum leverage because the employer has chosen you.</p>
  <h2>How much should I ask for above the offer?</h2>
  <p>10–20% above the offer is a reasonable starting anchor for most roles. In tech and finance, 20–30% asks are common because salary bands are wider. Research is critical — use Glassdoor, Levels.fyi (tech), LinkedIn Salary, and industry-specific surveys. Anchoring too low costs you money; anchoring too high is rarely disqualifying if done professionally.</p>
  <h2>What if they say the offer is &ldquo;non-negotiable&rdquo;?</h2>
  <p>This is often not true. Ask about negotiating non-salary components: signing bonus, equity/RSUs, start date (which affects year-end bonus timing), remote flexibility, accelerated review. Even in rigid pay bands, one-time bonuses are almost always negotiable. &ldquo;Is there flexibility on a signing bonus to bridge the gap?&rdquo; often works when base is locked.</p>
  <h2>Should I have a competing offer to negotiate?</h2>
  <p>A competing offer is the strongest negotiation leverage. Without one, rely on market data. Do not fabricate a competing offer — it can be verified and will damage trust. If you have a real offer, use it explicitly: &ldquo;I have a competing offer at $X — if you can match or beat it, I prefer to work here.&rdquo;</p>
  <h2>What if I get a raise at my current job during the negotiation?</h2>
  <p>A salary increase from your current employer doesn&rsquo;t obligate you to accept their offer. Use it as leverage with the prospective employer or to stay. Getting a competing internal offer and using it to negotiate externally is a legitimate and common tactic.</p>
</BaseLayout>
